1
00:00:00,352 --> 00:00:05,277
Okay, I'll give the talk.

2
00:00:05,338 --> 00:00:09,122
So, I'm going to start off in the right way.

3
00:00:10,243 --> 00:00:12,586
So I hope you've had a fruitful day.

4
00:00:12,606 --> 00:00:16,132
I do not say happy, but I hope it has been happy.

5
00:00:17,312 --> 00:00:24,181
Namo dasa bhagavato arahato sama sambodasa

6
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:47,462
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhasa Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhasa Homage to the Buddha, the Blessed, Noble and Fully Self-Awakened One.

7
00:00:47,481 --> 00:00:51,485
So we're looking at this, the Greater Discourse in Gozinga.

8
00:00:52,579 --> 00:00:53,801
We've been there before.

9
00:00:53,862 --> 00:01:01,634
You might remember that the Buddha approached three Arahats and asked them how they lived so well together, so peacefully together.

10
00:01:02,234 --> 00:01:06,400
So this is a case of the Gosingasala tree wood.

11
00:01:06,921 --> 00:01:16,115
Sala was the tree under which the Buddha was born and under which he died, I'm pretty sure.

12
00:01:16,917 --> 00:01:17,337
Anyway,

13
00:01:17,757 --> 00:01:22,724
There are a group of the elder monks here, Maha Moggallana and so on.

14
00:01:23,545 --> 00:01:34,540
And Maha Moggallana rose from meditation, went to the Venerable Maha Kasapa and said, friend, let's go and visit Venerable Sariputta and listen to the Dharma.

15
00:01:35,802 --> 00:01:46,498
And eventually they've got a group of monks, which includes Venerable Kasapa, Moggallana, Sariputta, Revata and Ananda.

16
00:01:47,203 --> 00:01:59,980
So they all come together to see Sariputta and Sariputta puts this question to Venerable Ananda.

17
00:02:03,084 --> 00:02:07,668
Friend Ananda, the Gosingasala wood is delightful.

18
00:02:07,909 --> 00:02:14,318
The night is moonlit, the sala trees are all in blossom and heavenly scents seem to be floating in the air.

19
00:02:14,877 --> 00:02:16,941
There's a bit of aesthetic joy.

20
00:02:18,625 --> 00:02:26,491
What kind of, it's got Bhikkhu, but to make it our, to make it good for us, we'll say meditator.

21
00:02:26,931 --> 00:02:32,075
What kind of meditator could illuminate this Gosinga Kala Sala Wood?

22
00:02:33,176 --> 00:02:43,406
And Ananda says, a meditator has learned much, remembers what he has learned and consolidates what he has learned.

23
00:02:44,127 --> 00:02:48,210
Such teachings as are good in the beginning, good in the middle and good in the end.

24
00:02:48,611 --> 00:02:55,318
with the right meaning and phrasing, and which affirm a holy life that is utterly perfect and pure.

25
00:02:55,998 --> 00:03:08,831
Such teachings as these he has learned much of, remembered, mastered verbally, investigated with the mind, and penetrated well with view, with understanding.

26
00:03:08,852 --> 00:03:14,417
And he teaches the Dharma to the four assemblies, so that's the monks and nuns, laywomen and laymen.

27
00:03:15,292 --> 00:03:25,384
with well-rounded and coherent statements and phrases for the eradication of the underlying tendencies, the old taints.

28
00:03:25,405 --> 00:03:43,087
So according to Ananda, what would illuminate any place, what would make a meditator illuminate any place they are would be their understanding, their intellectual understanding of the Dharma and the ability to teach it.

29
00:03:44,907 --> 00:03:52,275
So Venerable Ananda has spoken according to his own inspiration and then Sariputta turns to Revata.

30
00:03:53,015 --> 00:04:01,145
Revata says, a meditator delights in solitary meditation, takes delight in solitary meditation.

31
00:04:01,164 --> 00:04:10,435
He is devoted to internal serenity of mind, does not neglect meditation, possesses insight and dwells in empty huts.

32
00:04:10,495 --> 00:04:11,556
So Revata,

33
00:04:11,823 --> 00:04:21,375
who is the foremost of meditators, by the way, according to the Buddha, just wants us to meditate and try to find some empty huts.

34
00:04:21,855 --> 00:04:26,482
So I'm sure you've all got an empty hut at the end of your garden or something.

35
00:04:26,502 --> 00:04:40,259
So then Sariputta turns to Anuruddha and he says, with the divine eye, which is purified and surpasses the human, a meditator surveys the thousand worlds.

36
00:04:40,713 --> 00:04:54,576
Just as a man with good sight, when he has ascended to the upper palace chamber, might survey a thousand wheel rims, so too with the divine eye, which is purified and surpasses the human, the meditator surveys the south thousand worlds.

37
00:04:54,915 --> 00:05:03,430
So according to Anuruddha, we should develop the powers, these extraordinary powers.

38
00:05:04,591 --> 00:05:07,213
So then he turns to Maha Kasapa.

39
00:05:07,675 --> 00:05:20,071
Maha Kasapa was the one who led the convention after the Buddha died of the monks, of all the Arahats, where they decided what scriptures to remember.

40
00:05:21,632 --> 00:05:24,197
And he was known as a bit of a fierce man, as you will see.

41
00:05:24,237 --> 00:05:31,206
A meditator is a forest dweller himself and speaks in praise of forest dwellings.

42
00:05:31,709 --> 00:05:35,934
He's an arms eater himself and speaks in praise of eating arms food.

43
00:05:35,954 --> 00:05:41,360
He's a refuse rag wearer and speaks in praise of wearing refuse rags.

44
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:49,129
So remember in those days, they would even take the rags off the dead bodies, the corpses that were laid out on the charnel grounds.

45
00:05:50,490 --> 00:05:53,394
He's a triple robe wearer and speaks in praise of it.

46
00:05:53,454 --> 00:06:00,163
So if you're keeping the veneer very, very strictly, you should only have three robes, the lower, the upper,

47
00:06:00,430 --> 00:06:02,951
and the double robe, which is for cold nights.

48
00:06:04,334 --> 00:06:07,976
He has few wishes and speaks in praise of fewness of wishes.

49
00:06:08,516 --> 00:06:14,002
He is content and speaks in praise of contentment.

50
00:06:14,483 --> 00:06:17,665
He is secluded and speaks in praise of seclusion.

51
00:06:18,225 --> 00:06:22,569
He is aloof from society and speaks in praise of aloofness from society.

52
00:06:23,091 --> 00:06:27,855
He's energetic and speaks in praise of arousing energy

53
00:06:28,577 --> 00:06:58,112
attained to virtue himself speaks in praise of the attainment of virtue attained to concentration himself speaks in praise of concentration attained to wisdom himself speaks in praise of the attainment of wisdom he has attained deliverance and speaks in praise of the attainment of deliverance he's attained the knowledge and vision of deliverance and speaks in praise of it now that's that's a that's a tall order so if somebody

54
00:06:58,783 --> 00:07:03,668
Somebody who lives really very basically out there in the forest.

55
00:07:03,689 --> 00:07:09,576
You could do that, I suppose, in the garden and spends their lives trying to become fully liberated.

56
00:07:10,076 --> 00:07:14,701
All these people, all these monks, by the way, are liberated apart from Ananda.

57
00:07:14,901 --> 00:07:17,124
Ananda is not liberated.

58
00:07:17,144 --> 00:07:19,666
So now Sariputta turns to Moggallana.

59
00:07:21,949 --> 00:07:24,952
And Moggallana was the second disciple.

60
00:07:25,033 --> 00:07:26,714
So Sariputta, Moggallana,

61
00:07:27,033 --> 00:07:29,014
the two main disciples of the Buddha.

62
00:07:31,918 --> 00:07:50,199
What Moggallara suggests is that two meditators engage in the talk of the higher Dhamma and they question each other and each one and each being questioned by the other answers without foundering and their talk rolls on in accordance with the Dhamma.

63
00:07:50,701 --> 00:07:53,303
Now this, the word there is Abhidhamma.

64
00:07:53,324 --> 00:07:55,706
Now the Abhidhamma itself is the third

65
00:07:55,973 --> 00:07:57,516
of the baskets.

66
00:07:57,536 --> 00:08:06,069
So we talk about the Tipitika, there's the Vinaya, there's the Dharma, there's the discourses and the Abhidhamma, right?

67
00:08:06,089 --> 00:08:14,684
And the Abhidhamma was obviously developed later, but even during the Buddha's time, there was an attempt to systemize it.

68
00:08:15,324 --> 00:08:19,973
And you can see that at the end of the long discourses.

69
00:08:21,067 --> 00:08:25,172
So according to Moggallana, that's what we should be doing.

70
00:08:25,232 --> 00:08:27,935
We should be discussing the finer details of the Dharma.

71
00:08:30,038 --> 00:08:40,852
Now, Sariputta now, Moggallana turns to Sariputta, and Sariputta says, a meditator wields mastery over his mind.

72
00:08:40,972 --> 00:08:43,596
He does not let the mind wield mastery over him.

73
00:08:44,457 --> 00:08:48,643
In the morning, he abides in whatever abiding or attainment he wants.

74
00:08:48,977 --> 00:09:14,107
to abide to and to abide in during morning at midday he does the same and in the evening and then he gives a metaphor supposing a king or a king's minister with a chest full of variously colored garments in the morning he would put on whatever pair of garments he wanted to put on in the morning and so in the midday and so in the evening so too a monk

75
00:09:14,493 --> 00:09:18,258
A monk or meditator wields mastery over his mind.

76
00:09:18,357 --> 00:09:21,562
He does not let the mind wield mastery over him.

77
00:09:21,582 --> 00:09:29,312
So that's Sariputta saying that we should be in control of our heart-mind.

78
00:09:29,953 --> 00:09:36,803
Remember the word that's being translated here is citta, which refers to the combination of the heart and the mind.

79
00:09:37,524 --> 00:09:39,187
So we should have mastery over it, you see.

80
00:09:39,386 --> 00:09:42,171
Anyway, having had this discussion,

81
00:09:43,298 --> 00:09:46,081
So let's just go over that.

82
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:50,346
So they were in this beautiful wood.

83
00:09:51,927 --> 00:09:55,712
The night is moonlit and the sala trees are in blossom.

84
00:09:56,133 --> 00:09:58,316
Heavenly scents seem to float from the air.

85
00:09:59,397 --> 00:10:05,323
And according to Ananda, we should learn the Dharma and we should try and teach it.

86
00:10:06,125 --> 00:10:13,153
And according to, where are we, Anaruda,

87
00:10:13,437 --> 00:10:42,600
uh which we should be um we should be meditating all the time that's right excuse me solitary meditation and according to um what's his name where is he i've gone to anurudha i've lost my place here and i'm getting all confused where's his name cast uh anurudha yes uh we should be developing the powers according to um

88
00:10:43,727 --> 00:10:52,116
Mahakasyapa, we should be living very sparsely and in the forests, devoting our time to meditation.

89
00:10:52,157 --> 00:10:58,043
According to Moggallana, we should be talking to each other about the finer parts of the Dharma.

90
00:10:58,504 --> 00:11:02,447
And according to Sariputta, we should be in control of our minds.

91
00:11:02,788 --> 00:11:07,974
So now they approach the Buddha, you see, and they tell him what they've been talking about.

92
00:11:08,794 --> 00:11:10,917
And then Venerable Sariputta asks the Buddha,

93
00:11:11,403 --> 00:11:14,025
Venerable sir, which one of us has spoken well?

94
00:11:15,167 --> 00:11:16,828
So they want a bit of praise, I suppose.

95
00:11:18,750 --> 00:11:23,376
So the Buddha says, you have all spoken well, Sariputta, each in his own way.

96
00:11:24,158 --> 00:11:30,745
Hear also from me what kind of monk or meditator would illuminate this Krasinga Sala Wood.

97
00:11:31,605 --> 00:11:40,937
Here, when a meditator has returned from his alms round after his meal, he sits down, folds his leg crosswise,

98
00:11:41,187 --> 00:11:45,894
sets his body erect and establishing mindfulness in front of him resolves.

99
00:11:46,735 --> 00:11:47,296
Wait for this.

100
00:11:48,177 --> 00:11:56,190
I shall not break this sitting posture until through not clinging, my mind is liberated from the taints.

101
00:11:57,893 --> 00:12:05,605
So now this of course is what the Buddha did right at the end of all that training, where he came to a position where he thought, look, it's now or never.

102
00:12:06,086 --> 00:12:09,591
And he went to sit under the Bodhi tree, the religious fig tree,

103
00:12:09,892 --> 00:12:15,198
And made that determination that either he would crack this problem or die.

104
00:12:15,739 --> 00:12:18,101
And he sat there, it seems, for six hours.

105
00:12:18,582 --> 00:12:21,986
And lucky for him, lucky for us, he made it.

106
00:12:22,006 --> 00:12:25,652
So that's the Buddha's advice.

107
00:12:26,232 --> 00:12:32,961
And what the commentaries say is that all the previous ones are really relating to people who've already made it.

108
00:12:33,522 --> 00:12:36,725
For the Buddha here is talking about somebody who hasn't made it.

109
00:12:37,009 --> 00:12:39,692
and the determination needed to make it.

110
00:12:40,354 --> 00:12:47,740
So that's the discourse on the Maha Gosinga, the great Buddha Gosinga.

111
00:12:49,923 --> 00:13:01,395
So I hope you found that somewhat inspiring and that you will, of course, make this great effort to sit from now on until you're fully liberated and we can start shortly.

112
00:13:02,115 --> 00:13:02,697
Very good.

